Bounds checks on what userspace hands the VBC DAI and the UMP9620 codec (the same changes as the mainline port,
mu300-linux FINDINGS 188; applied last, after the other patches).

* Every enum put checked ucontrol->value.integer.value[0] - a long, which on arm64 is enumerated.item[0] and item[1]
  together - against the number of items, and then used enumerated.item[0] as the index. A write with item[1]'s top
  bit set made the long negative, passed the check, and item[0] indexed the text and setting arrays out of bounds:
  pr_info/sprintf of a wild char * (a read past the array, and with "%s_%u" into char buf[128] on the stack, a
  stack overflow), and writes past vbc_codec's arrays. The check is on enumerated.item[0] now, the value used. 5.4
  has no CONFIG_SND_CTL_INPUT_VALIDATION (mainline checks it in the core as well).
* A profile blob (/dev/vbc_turningN, or a /lib/firmware profile) was trusted: its header was copied from a buffer
  that could be shorter than it, the data buffer of an earlier, smaller profile was kept and the new, larger one
  copied into it (a heap overflow), and reading the profile back copied the new header's size out of the old buffer
  (a heap read past it). vbc_profile_store() now checks the magic and the sizes (num_mode * len_mode, overflow,
  4 MiB), allocates exactly what the header says, and a bad blob changes nothing; under load_mutex, which the apply
  and read paths hold.
* The "... Profile Select" controls choose a mode that indexed the data buffer without a check against num_mode
  (a read past it): checked before the data is used.
* The turning device's read and write took any size from userspace for vmalloc() and handed back data_size bytes of
  an uninitialised buffer: bounded, zeroed, and only the bytes filled are copied out.

